# Inventions and Discoveries: The Rapid-Fire Science GK File

> Who invented the telephone? Who discovered penicillin? Which
> Indian discovered the effect that won Asia its first science
> Nobel? Inventions-and-discoveries questions are the fastest marks
> in the paper — pure association, one name per thing — and the
> examiners' traps come from exactly two habits: swapping the two
> co-discoverers and moving a discovery to its populariser.

## 1. The Communication and Transport Set

[definition]
An **invention** is a device or process created by human ingenuity
(the telephone); a **discovery** is a finding of something that
already existed in nature (penicillin's mould, X-rays). Exams use
both words loosely, but a few marks have turned on reading which
one the question actually says.
[/definition]

| Invention | Inventor | Year |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Printing press (movable type, Europe) | Johannes Gutenberg | c. 1440 |
| Steam engine (improved, with separate condenser) | James Watt | 1769 |
| Telegraph | Samuel Morse (code) / Cooke-Wheatstone | 1837–44 |
| Telephone | Alexander Graham Bell | 1876 |
| Radio (wireless telegraphy) | Guglielmo Marconi | 1895 |
| Incandescent light bulb (practical) | Thomas Alva Edison | 1879 |
| Motor car (petrol, practical) | Karl Benz | 1885–86 |
| Diesel engine | Rudolf Diesel | 1892–97 |
| Airplane (powered, controlled flight) | Wilbur and Orville Wright | 1903 |
| Television (mechanical) | John Logie Baird | 1925–26 |
| Helicopter (practical, single-rotor) | Igor Sikorsky | 1939 |
| Turbojet / jet engine | Frank Whittle / Hans von Ohain | 1930s–40s |
| World Wide Web | Tim Berners-Lee | 1989 (CERN) |
| Linux kernel | Linus Torvalds | 1991 |

The Bell question's favourite trap is **Elisha Gray**, who filed a
caveat for a similar device the same day in 1876; the radio trap is
**Nikola Tesla** (whose priority the US Supreme Court acknowledged
in 1943). Papers rarely go that deep, but "Marconi — radio" and
"Baird — television" are the safe anchors.

## 2. The Physics Set — Rays, Particles, and Relativity

| Discovery / invention | Scientist | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Law of gravitation, mechanics, optics | Isaac Newton | Principia, 1687 |
| Laws of electromagnetic induction | Michael Faraday | 1831 — the dynamo's basis |
| X-rays | Wilhelm Röntgen | 1895; first Physics Nobel, 1901 |
| Radioactivity | Henri Becquerel; Marie and Pierre Curie isolated radium/polonium | 1896–98 |
| The electron | J.J. Thomson | 1897 |
| The proton | Ernest Rutherford | 1917–19 |
| The neutron | James Chadwick | 1932 |
| Special and general relativity | Albert Einstein | 1905 / 1915 |
| Quantum theory (quanta) | Max Planck | 1900 |
| Raman effect | C.V. Raman | 1928 — Asia's first science Nobel |
| Chandrasekhar limit | Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar | Physics Nobel 1983 |
| Photoelectric effect (explanation) | Einstein | The work behind his 1921 Nobel, not relativity |
| Barometer | Evangelista Torricelli | 1643 |
| Thermometer scales | Fahrenheit, Celsius | 1714 / 1742 |
| Telescope (astronomical use) | Galileo Galilei | 1609 |
| Dynamite | Alfred Nobel | 1867 |

The subatomic ladder — **Thomson (electron, 1897), Rutherford
(proton), Chadwick (neutron, 1932)** — is the single most reliable
triple in science GK, and the examiner's move is always a rotation:
they give you the particle and want the physicist, or the physicist
and want the year order.

## 3. The Chemistry, Biology, and Medicine Set

| Discovery / invention | Scientist | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Periodic table's periodic law | Dmitri Mendeleev | 1869; predicted missing elements |
| Structure of the benzene ring | August Kekulé | 1865 |
| Pasteurisation, germ theory | Louis Pasteur | — |
| Vaccination (smallpox, first scientific) | Edward Jenner | 1796 — "father of immunology" |
| Antiseptic surgery | Joseph Lister | carbolic acid, 1860s |
| Penicillin | Alexander Fleming | 1928 (developed into a drug by Florey and Chain) |
| Polio vaccine (inactivated) | Jonas Salk | 1955 (oral: Albert Sabin) |
| DNA's double helix | Watson, Crick, with Rosalind Franklin's X-ray data | 1953 |
| Evolution by natural selection | Charles Darwin | *Origin of Species*, 1859 |
| Genetics' laws | Gregor Mendel | 1865 (pea plants) |
| Blood circulation | William Harvey | 1628 |
| Blood groups | Karl Landsteiner | ABO system, 1901 |
| Anaesthesia (ether demonstration) | William Morton | 1846 |
| Microscope pioneer / microbiology | Antonie van Leeuwenhoek | animalcules |
| Test-tube baby (world's first) | Patrick Steptoe and Robert Edwards | Louise Brown, 1978 |

The Fleming trap is **Florey and Chain**, who turned the mould's
juice into the medicine — all three shared the 1945 Nobel. The
Darwin trap is **Alfred Russel Wallace**, whose letter forced the
1858 joint presentation. And the Jenner trap is Pasteur: Jenner
made the first vaccine, but Pasteur gave the field its name and its
science.

## 4. The Indian Column and the Computer Age

| First | Indian name | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Zero's arithmetic rules (earliest formal treatment) | Brahmagupta | 628 CE, *Brahmasphutasiddhanta* |
| Place-value decimal system | Aryabhata (and Bakhshali-era practice) | Aryabhatiya, 499 CE |
| Raman effect | C.V. Raman | 1928; National Science Day marks its announcement |
| Number theory / partition theory | Srinivasa Ramanujan | 1729 taxicab story; National Mathematics Day on his birthday |
| Chandrasekhar limit (white-dwarf mass) | Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar | 1983 Nobel; the name behind the Chandra X-ray Observatory |
| India's first satellite | Aryabhata (ISRO, named for the mathematician) | Launched 1975 |
| Analytical engine (concept) | Charles Babbage | "Father of the computer" |
| First programmable electronic computer (ENIAC era) | Eckert and Mauchly | 1946 |
| Difference engine | Babbage | — |
| Programming languages / compilers | Grace Hopper | COBOL lineage |
| Analytical engine's first programmer | Ada Lovelace | The first algorithm for a machine |

The double Aryabhata — the fifth-century mathematician and the 1975
satellite named for him — supplies the paper's favourite "both are
the same name" question. Ramanujan and Raman supply the other
confusable pair, separated by discipline and by day: Mathematics
Day (22 December) versus Science Day (28 February).

[example]
A rotation question in action: "The proton was discovered by —" with
J.J. Thomson and Chadwick as the trap options. Thomson found the
electron (1897), Rutherford the proton, Chadwick the neutron
(1932). The examiner then rotates: same options, "the electron?"
Thomson; "the neutron?" Chadwick. One memorised line — Thomson,
Rutherford, Chadwick; electron, proton, neutron — answers three
questions in the paper.
[/example]

[summary]
Communication and transport: Gutenberg (printing), Watt (steam
engine), Morse (telegraph), Bell (telephone, 1876), Marconi
(radio), Edison (practical bulb), Benz (motor car), Wright brothers
(airplane, 1903), Baird (television), Sikorsky (helicopter),
Berners-Lee (WWW, 1989). Physics: Newton, Faraday (induction),
Röntgen (X-rays, first Nobel), Becquerel and the Curies
(radioactivity), Thomson-Rutherford-Chadwick (electron-proton-
neutron), Einstein (relativity, but Nobel for the photoelectric
effect), Planck (quanta), Raman (1928). Chemistry and life:
Mendeleev (1869), Kekulé (benzene), Pasteur, Jenner (first
vaccine), Fleming (penicillin, with Florey and Chain), Salk
(polio), Watson-Crick-Franklin (DNA), Darwin (with Wallace),
Mendel, Landsteiner (blood groups). India: Brahmagupta (zero),
Aryabhata (place value; and the 1975 satellite), Ramanujan,
Chandrasekhar. Read rotations: the particle ladder and the
vaccine–pasteurisation pair are where the marks hide.
[/summary]
